<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Bosnia and Herzegovina</h1><p class="st-meta">1992 CE – present · capital Sarajevo</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Southeastern European state independent from Yugoslavia since 1992, which fought the Bosnian War until 1995 and is divided into two entities.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Bosnia and Herzegovina, one of the republics of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/socialist-federal-republic-of-yugoslavia">Socialist Federal Republic of Yugoslavia</a>, held multi-party elections in November 1990 that produced a coalition of three ethnically based parties. Independence was declared on 3 March 1992 after a referendum boycotted by most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, in which 99.7 per cent of voters backed independence. It was recognised on 6 April 1992 and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> on 22 May 1992.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnian-war">Bosnian War</a> followed, with Bosnian Serb forces supported from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> taking much of the country and carrying out ethnic cleansing, which culminated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srebrenica">Srebrenica</a> massacre of more than 8,000 Bosniak men and boys in July 1995, ruled a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a> by the International Criminal Tribunal for the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>. Bosniak and Croat forces also committed war crimes, mostly during the Croat-Bosniak War, which ended with the Washington Agreement of March 1994. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dayton-agreement">Dayton Agreement</a> ended the war and created two entities, the Federation of Bosnia and Herzegovina and Republika Srpska, with a High Representative supervising civilian peace implementation.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>The state has a presidency with one member from each of the three main ethnic groups and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bicameral-legislature">bicameral legislature</a>, while most authority rests with the entities. Protests began in Tuzla on 4 February 2014 over unemployment and unpaid wages. In 2021 Milorad Dodik spoke in favour of secession by Republika Srpska, and on 15 December 2022 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> recognised the country as a candidate for accession.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/bosnia-and-herzegovina.jpg" alt="Bosnia and Herzegovina" width="330" height="165"><figcaption>Image: Mladen Kolobarić · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Bosnia_and_Herzegovina.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1992 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sarajevo</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Islam, Orthodox, Catholic</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Bosnian, Serbian, Croatian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bosnia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=2009&amp;focus=bosnia-and-herzegovina" aria-label="Bosnia and Herzegovina on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: where Bosnia and Herzegovina lay"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4290 3791L4424 3723"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4424 3723h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4290 3791h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4290 3791h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Sarajevo. <a href="/atlas?year=2009&amp;focus=bosnia-and-herzegovina">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
